updates={
'def-it-wiretap-channel':'A finite nonempty DM wiretap channel W(y,z|x) is row stochastic and used as its n-fold product. A uniform private message M has a stochastic encoder P_Xn|M using private randomness independent of M and channel noise, and Bob has a decoder from Y^n (erasure allowed). Eve receives all of Z^n and the public code description. Reliability is separately uniform-message average or per-message maximal error; secrecy criterion is specified by the consuming theorem.',
'def-it-weak-and-strong-secrecy':'For uniform finite messages M_n and Eve observation Z^n under a specified sequence of codes, weak secrecy means I(M_n;Z^n)/n ->0, and strong secrecy means I(M_n;Z^n)->0, in nats. These definitions use unnormalized mutual information; TV, semantic or per-message distance are separately named criteria and are not identified with this limit without a proved conversion.',
'def-it-finite-source-privacy-utility-region':'Fix a finite joint source law P_XY. For every finite nonempty output alphabet Z and stochastic kernel K(z|y), form P_XYZ=P_XY K, so X-Y-Z. Define the closure of the union of pairs (I(X;Z),I(Y;Z)) in R²: first coordinate is leakage, second is the explicitly chosen relevant-information utility. This definition does not use a distortion or an unspecified utility.',
'def-it-quantum-source-and-compression-code':'A finite nonzero system A and state rho define the IID source rho^tensor n. An (n,M) compression code consists of CPTP encoder E:A^n->C with dim C=M>=1 and decoder D:C->A^n. With a purification phi of rho^tensor n, squared entanglement fidelity is <phi|(id_R tensor D E)(phi)|phi>; this is independent of purification by finite isometric equivalence. A rate uses log M/n in the stated units and reliability means this fidelity tends to one. Ensemble average pure-state fidelity is a separate named criterion.',
'def-it-quantum-channel-block-code-and-error':'For a finite-dimensional channel N:A->B, an (n,K) quantum entanglement-transmission code consists of CPTP E:L(K)->A^n and D:B^n->L(K). Its squared entanglement fidelity is <Phi_K|(id_R tensor D N^tensor n E)(Phi_K)|Phi_K>, with maximally entangled normalized Phi_K. Classical codes instead assign a state to each uniform message and use a POVM, with average or maximal message error explicitly selected. Private classical encoders may use private randomization. EA classical codes additionally share a finite state independent of the message, growing with n without restriction, and send only through N; Bob measures B^n with his retained share. These tasks have distinct capacities.',
'def-it-block-channel-code-and-decoder':'For a specified W_n, n>=1,M>=1, an (n,M) code has uniform message J in [M], a deterministic encoder word x_j or a stated stochastic encoder kernel independent of channel noise, and a measurable decoder test family d_j(y) in [0,1] with sum_j d_j(y)<=1. Leftover probability is erasure and counts as error. Deterministic decoding is the special case disjoint indicator tests; private randomization must be explicitly permitted by the theorem.',
'def-it-channel-error-criteria':'For an (n,M) code define e_j=1-integral d_j(y) W_n(dy|x_j), averaged over its encoder law if stochastic. Average error is M^-1 sum_j e_j and maximal error is max_j e_j. M*_av(n,epsilon) and M*_max(n,epsilon) are separate suprema of permitted code sizes under these constraints; in finite DMC cases with epsilon<1 the maximum is finite and attained under the stated model.',
'def-it-channel-capacity':'For a fixed channel sequence, input constraints and code/randomization model, R>=0 is achievable under the chosen av or max criterion if there are fixed-length codes for all sufficiently large n with liminf ln M_n/n>=R and error tending to zero. Capacity is the extended supremum of achievable R, with nats/use default. Memorylessness, stationarity and cost are extra premises, not consequences of this definition.',
'def-it-channel-cost-constraint':'A nonnegative measurable c:X->[0,infinity] induces block cost sum_t c(x_t), an extended measurable function. At budget P>=0 the admissible one-letter laws satisfy integral c dP_X<=P. An optimization or coding theorem requiring a feasible law assumes this family is nonempty explicitly.',
'def-it-codeword-and-expected-cost-criteria':'A peak block-cost code has sum_t c(x_t)<=nP for every deterministic codeword (almost surely under each message encoder kernel). An expected-over-codebook code requires the expectation over the uniform message and private encoder randomness to be <=nP. These constraints are distinct at finite blocklength; neither is called a pointwise per-symbol peak constraint.',
'def-it-awgn-channel-and-cost':'Real AWGN has Y_i=X_i+Z_i, iid independent Z_i~N(0,sigma²), 0<sigma²<infinity, and cost x². Complex AWGN has independent circular Gaussian noise with E|Z|²=sigma² and cost |x|², a separate one-complex-symbol normalization. Fix finite P>=0 and state peak block or expected codebook power; memorylessness and no feedback apply unless a separate feedback model is given.',
'def-it-differential-entropy-finite-second-moment':'For an absolutely continuous law P on R^d with finite second moment, choose a nonsingular standard Gaussian density g and define h(P)=-D(P||g dx)-E_P ln g(X), in [-infinity,infinity). The Gaussian cross entropy is finite, so no infinity-minus-infinity arises. Changing to any other nonsingular Gaussian reference gives the same value by the finite integrable quadratic log-density difference. It equals -integral f ln f with its justified extended integral; this is distinct from general law entropy I(X;X).',
'def-it-channel-dispersion':'For every finite-DMC input P, define V(P,W)=sum_(x:P_x>0) P_x Var_Wx[ln(W(Y|x)/(PW)(Y))]. Each charged cell has (PW)(y)>0; zero cells are omitted. This finite conditional variance is measured in nats². On the compact CAID set Pi define V_min=min_Pi V and V_max=max_Pi V. Their use at different fixed error levels is a theorem-specific selection; the proved normal approximation here uses epsilon<1/2 and V_min>0.',
'def-it-common-output-two-way-channel':'A finite DM-TWC has W(y1,y2|x1,x2), independent private uniform terminal messages, inputs X_ji=f_ji(M_j,Y_j^(i-1)), and terminal j decodes the other message using its own message and local outputs. A common-output specialization instead has one Y delivered identically to both terminals, W(y|x1,x2); an arbitrary joint-output TWC is not common-output.',
'def-it-discrete-memoryless-relay-channel':'A finite nonempty DM relay channel has row-stochastic W(y2,y3|x1,x2). Node 1 holds the uniform message, node 2 holds no independent message and uses X_2i=f_2i(Y_2^(i-1)), node 3 decodes from Y_3^n. Source input is message-only in the no-feedback model. Any additional source/relay feedback or common-message access is an explicitly different topology.'}
